[0] To state Faraday's law of electromagnetic induction precisely and quantitatively, we need a new physical quantity: **magnetic flux**.
[1] Consider a plane closed wire loop in a uniform magnetic field. Its area is $S$.
    break: {"before":"To state Faraday's law of electromagnetic induction precisely and quantitatively, we need a new physical quantity: **magnetic flux**.","after":"Consider a plane closed wire loop in a uniform magnetic field. Its area is $S$."}
[2] The normal $\vec{n}$ to the plane of the loop and the magnetic induction $\vec{B}$ make an angle $\alpha$ (Figure 283).
    break: {"before":"Consider a plane closed wire loop in a uniform magnetic field. Its area is $S$.","after":"The normal $\\vec{n}$ to the plane of the loop and the magnetic induction $\\vec{B}$ make an angle $\\alpha$ (Figure 283)."}
[3] **The product of the magnitude of the magnetic induction, the area $S$, and the cosine of the angle between $\vec{B}$ and $\vec{n}$, denoted by $\alpha$, is called the magnetic flux through the area $S$ and is denoted by $\Phi$**.
    break: {"before":"The normal $\\vec{n}$ to the plane of the loop and the magnetic induction $\\vec{B}$ make an angle $\\alpha$ (Figure 283).","after":"**The product of the magnitude of the magnetic induction, the area $S$, and the cosine of the angle between $\\vec{B}$ and $\\vec{n}$, denoted by $\\alpha$, is called the magnetic flux through the area $S$ and is denoted by $\\Phi$**."}
[4] The magnetic flux is given by the following expression:
$\Phi=BS\cos\alpha$. (12-1)
    break: {"before":"**The product of the magnitude of the magnetic induction, the area $S$, and the cosine of the angle between $\\vec{B}$ and $\\vec{n}$, denoted by $\\alpha$, is called the magnetic flux through the area $S$ and is denoted by $\\Phi$**.","after":"The magnetic flux is given by the following expression:\n$\\Phi=BS\\cos\\alpha$. (12-1)"}
[5] The product $B\cos\alpha=B_n$ is the projection of the magnetic induction onto the normal to the plane of the loop.
The magnetic flux can therefore also be written as follows:
$\Phi=B_nS$. (12-2)
    break: {"before":"The magnetic flux is given by the following expression:\n$\\Phi=BS\\cos\\alpha$. (12-1)","after":"The product $B\\cos\\alpha=B_n$ is the projection of the magnetic induction onto the normal to the plane of the loop.\nThe magnetic flux can therefore also be written as follows:\n$\\Phi=B_nS$. (12-2)"}
[6] The magnetic induction $\vec{B}$ describes the magnetic field at each point in space.
    break: {"before":"The product $B\\cos\\alpha=B_n$ is the projection of the magnetic induction onto the normal to the plane of the loop.\nThe magnetic flux can therefore also be written as follows:\n$\\Phi=B_nS$. (12-2)","after":"The magnetic induction $\\vec{B}$ describes the magnetic field at each point in space."}
[7] Magnetic flux depends not only on $\vec{B}$, but also on the area of the loop and its orientation relative to the magnetic field lines.
    break: {"before":"The magnetic induction $\\vec{B}$ describes the magnetic field at each point in space.","after":"Magnetic flux depends not only on $\\vec{B}$, but also on the area of the loop and its orientation relative to the magnetic field lines."}
[8] Magnetic flux can be interpreted as a directed count of magnetic field lines passing through the area $S$, with signs defined relative to the chosen normal to the plane of the loop.
    break: {"before":"Magnetic flux depends not only on $\\vec{B}$, but also on the area of the loop and its orientation relative to the magnetic field lines.","after":"Magnetic flux can be interpreted as a directed count of magnetic field lines passing through the area $S$, with signs defined relative to the chosen normal to the plane of the loop."}
[9] Suppose that the number of magnetic field lines crossing a unit area perpendicular to the vector $\vec{B}$ is defined to equal the vector's magnitude $B$, with crossings along the chosen normal counted positively and crossings against it counted negatively.
    break: {"before":"Magnetic flux can be interpreted as a directed count of magnetic field lines passing through the area $S$, with signs defined relative to the chosen normal to the plane of the loop.","after":"Suppose that the number of magnetic field lines crossing a unit area perpendicular to the vector $\\vec{B}$ is defined to equal the vector's magnitude $B$, with crossings along the chosen normal counted positively and crossings against it counted negatively."}
[10] Under this convention, magnetic flux equals the directed count of magnetic field lines passing through the area $S$.
    break: {"before":"Suppose that the number of magnetic field lines crossing a unit area perpendicular to the vector $\\vec{B}$ is defined to equal the vector's magnitude $B$, with crossings along the chosen normal counted positively and crossings against it counted negatively.","after":"Under this convention, magnetic flux equals the directed count of magnetic field lines passing through the area $S$."}
[11] Reversing the chosen normal reverses the sign of the flux. In the uniform-field, plane-loop case considered here, a geometrical count that ignores crossing direction corresponds only to the magnitude of the flux and does not change when the chosen normal is reversed.
